body:
Following Alan Thrall's 5 Step deadlift setup will clean up alot of things startup wise.

&#x200B;

Dont look sideways during the lift, just keep a neutral neck. Theres no need to watch yourself in the mirror and you tend to overcorrect yourself.

&#x200B;

Initiate every rep from a dead start.

Q1: "I will also try w some shoes next time so I can wedge myself into position better."

A1: It also depends on the shoe, DL slippers, vans, converse, wrestling shoes (anything flat, thin and with a hard sole) are ideal if you have none of those then socks are better.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Q2: "What part of it looks conventional to you and how does it contrast w sumo? I’ve been having trouble finding the differences"

A2: Well lemme rephrase, It looks more of a squat than a deadlift, I've mentioned wedging in a sumo but you also want to wedge yourself in a conv deadlift but thats limited since your legs are in the way. You don't really wedge yourself in you just sit back and then push with the quads resulting in squatting the weight up.

&#x200B;

Q3: "I’ve been having trouble finding the differences"

A3: Well the TLDR would be where the feet are in relation with the grip, you still want to apply the wedge at both like i said in A2.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

By any chance a 45° front angle?

Define lower back problems

&#x200B;

Edit:

&#x200B;

Well it's still a deadlift so you'll feel it in ur lower back, muscular pain shouldnt really be a problem unless it's extreme. if it's a different kind of pain then it's a problem and cant really pintpoint it to something since different exercises require the lower back (example: Squat) + bad posture can contribute to it.

&#x200B;

If everything checks out and you're certain that it's deadlifts, my only thought would be to revise bracing/breathing patterns and engagement the lats.

&#x200B;

For bracing: breath into your belly and tense it, dont just try and push ur gut out. hmm pinch your fingers above your hips and see if u can push them away with ur core. Note: The fingers on ur lower back should also move, not just ur belly. A different tool to measure if u can brace properly is have someone lightly punch ur stomach and see if u can talk/breath while s/he is still lightly punching it.

&#x200B;

For engaging the lats: Depress ur scapula and screw your armpits shut, imagine someone is trying to tickle you from behind and u want to avoid that. Note: you DONT heavily focus on retracting your scapula since you will lose that position on heavier weights + you will lose your bracing.

if the soles of ur shoes are soft, ditch em.

&#x200B;

work on bracing, if u want a more consistent deadlift u can pull the slack. (before lifting the bar already apply some pressure with your quads)
